njugation (ufmylation) system is conserved in multicellular organisms. Ufm1 is known to covalently attach with cellular protein(s) via a specific E1-activating","Publisher":{"Name":"InterPro"}}},{"ObtainedBy":"submission","PublicationDate":"11-02-2026","LinkProvider":{"Name":"Europe PMC"},"RelationshipType":{"Name":"IsReferencedBy"},"Source":{"Type":{"Name":"literature"},"Identifier":{"ID":"32160526","IDScheme":"PMID"}},"Target":{"Type":{"Name":"dataset"},"Identifier":{"ID":"IPR056761","IDScheme":"InterPro","IDURL":"http://www.ebi.ac.uk/interpro/entry/IPR056761"},"Title":"['This domain is found C-terminal in human E3 UFM1-protein ligase 1 (Ufl1) and similar eukaryotic sequences. This domain is predicted to adopt a globular structure consisting of an array of α-helices. The ubiquitin fold modifier 1 (Ufm1) is the most recently discovered ubiquitin-like modifier whose conjugation (ufmylation) system is conserved in multicellular organisms. Ufm1 is known to covalently attach with cellular protein(s) via a specific E1-activating enzyme (Uba5), an E2-conjugating enzym","Publisher":{"Name":"InterPro"}}},{"ObtainedBy":"submission","PublicationDate":"11-02-2026","LinkProvider":{"Name":"Europe PMC"},"RelationshipType":{"Name":"IsReferencedBy"},"Source":{"Type":{"Name":"literature"},"Identifier":{"ID":"32160526","IDScheme":"PMID"}},"Target":{"Type":{"Name":"dataset"},"Identifier":{"ID":"IPR056579","IDScheme":"InterPro","IDURL":"http://www.ebi.ac.uk/interpro/entry/IPR056579"},"Title":"['This entry represents the N-terminal region of E3 UFM1-protein ligase 1 (Ufl1) and similar sequences mainly found in animals and plants. The ubiquitin fold modifier 1 (Ufm1) is the most recently discovered ubiquitin-like modifier whose conjugation (ufmylation) system is conserved in multicellular organisms. Ufm1 is known to covalently attach with cellular protein(s) via a specific E1-activating enzyme (Uba5), an E2-conjugating enzyme (Ufc1), and a E3-ligating enzyme (Ufl1) [[PMID:20018847]]. U","Publisher":{"Name":"InterPro"}}}]}}]}]}}